From f7338a7116b21b4e0e086ea5435f09c857dca4bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:22:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(design): DESIGN-01 - a photograph of the floor, not a
 diagram of one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/design painted its preview with three repeating-linear-gradient layers over a
two-hex-colour ground, laid down with rotateX. It cost nothing on first paint
and it read as exactly what it was: a diagram of a floor, shown to somebody
deciding how to spend five figures. Meanwhile the camera visualiser had real
board geometry, real species pigment and photographs of our own floors, and
could not be used here, because compositeFloor takes its light from the
photograph it is given and /design has no photograph.

So this synthesises the room. A floor plane under a real perspective camera -
forty-eight inch lens height, eighteen degrees down, a 28mm-equivalent field of
view - lit by one window, with the wall it runs into. Everything below the
horizon goes through the same samplePattern and woodColourFrom the visualiser
uses, at the same measured mip level, from the same photographed tile. The
floor a person designs here and the floor they later point their camera at are
rendered by the same code from the same wood, which is the only way the two can
ever agree.

WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE TILES, WHICH IS MOST OF THIS COMMIT

Wiring the photographs in looked like crumpled foil rather than wood, and the
mip fix in CAM-02 did not account for it. Four separate defects, each measured
before it was believed:

  1. THE GRAIN DID NOT RUN ALONG THE BOARD. Of the five tiles shipped, exactly
     one had its grain on the axis the renderer maps to the length of a plank.
     White oak's ran across it. Walnut's and maple's ran at forty-five degrees,
     because those photographs are of a chevron floor and a herringbone floor.
     The renderer duly painted oak with the grain running sideways across every
     board. The build script now MEASURES the angle - a structure tensor, whose
     principal direction is across the grain - rotates, and measures again; if
     the second reading is further from vertical than the first, the sign was
     wrong and it flips. Worst tile in the new set is 2.4 degrees off. No sign
     convention is reasoned about anywhere in that file, which is how it
     survived PIL and sharp disagreeing about which way a positive angle turns.

  2. THE TILES WERE SQUARE, AND A BOARD IS NOT. build-grain.py resized every
     crop to 512x512, which squashes a tall crop and takes the grain with it,
     and which makes a tile a few inches long. A board is four feet long, so
     that tile repeated six or eight times down every one of them - and that
     repeat is what the eye reads as a ripple running across the floor. The one
     accidentally-tall tile in the old set, hickory, was the only one that did
     not wave. Tiles are now cut three to one ALONG the grain.

  3. THE TILE'S SIZE IN INCHES WAS A CONSTANT, AND WRONG. Ten inches, for every
     tile, on both axes. The tiles are neither square nor the same size as each
     other: hickory is 5.8 inches across and 17.5 along, walnut 2.5 and 7.4.
     Each tile now declares its own, estimated from the board's share of the
     frame against a seven-inch wide plank, and the manifest records the basis
     as an estimate rather than pretending to a measurement.

  4. THE DETAIL RATIO WAS BUILT IN LINEAR LIGHT AND APPLIED TO GAMMA-ENCODED
     COLOUR. fastLuminance decodes sRGB through a 2.4 power. The ratio it
     produced was then multiplied straight into channels that are still
     encoded, so a grain feature ten percent darker than its board came out
     twenty-four percent darker on the floor. That is the rippling, and its
     amplitude was set by nothing but the gamma curve. Tone is now measured in
     the space the multiply happens in.

Three things were suspected and measured NOT to be the cause, and are recorded
because each cost an hour: the detail amplitude (0.86 to 1.16 at the tenth and
ninetieth percentiles across all five tiles, nowhere near the clamp); the mip
filter (the pyramid dumps cleanly, level by level); and the photograph's own
lighting, which is divided out at build time and had been all along - though
at a radius set as a fraction of the tile, which on a tall tile meant an inch
and a half and removed the plank-scale grain along with the window. It is now
three inches, in inches.

PERFORMANCE, BECAUSE A PREVIEW THAT BLOCKS IS A BROKEN PAGE

A photographic plate at preview size started at 287ms. That is 287ms in which
the page ignores every click - on a phone, closer to a second, which is
indistinguishable from broken. Three changes, measured at 640x480:

  · grainAt allocated two closures and an Rgb per pixel, three hundred
    thousand a frame and two million a second at video rate. Written out flat.
  · The photograph modulates BRIGHTNESS and never colour, and tone is linear in
    the channels while bilinear interpolation is linear in its samples, so the
    two commute. Each mip level now carries a tone plane and the renderer does
    one interpolation instead of three. 287ms -> 190ms.
  · woodColourFrom did a Map.get per pixel for a board that does not change for
    tens or hundreds of pixels at a stretch. Single-entry memo in front of it.
  · Two Math.exp per pixel in the plate's light, for a soft pool whose exact
    profile nobody can name. Rational falloff. 190ms -> 180ms.

The remaining 180ms is spent a band of rows at a time inside a 10ms budget,
yielding between - so the floor fills in over two or three frames and the page
never drops one. A test asserts the banded render is byte-identical to the
single-pass one across all four patterns, because a band that depended on the
last one would show seams wherever an animation frame happened to end.

The drawn floor goes up first, needing no photograph and costing a third as
much, and the photographic pass replaces it when the tile has decoded. If the
canvas is unavailable, JavaScript is off, or the tile never arrives, the CSS
plane is still in the markup underneath and is what a person gets. Nothing was
removed; a complete page has an upgrade laid over it.

WHITE ASH, WHICH WAS ALREADY A PRODUCT

It has two floors in app/data/floors.ts with copy, six photographs in
public/gallery, a dossier at /guides/white-ash-flooring-toronto, an entry in
the topic map, published shrinkage coefficients in lib/wood/species.ts, and a
tone in SpeciesSwatch whose comment reads, in as many words, "White Ash has no
configurator entry". Six species dossiers, five species on the page where a
person designs a floor. Every figure the catalogue entry needs already existed
somewhere in this repository; none of it is invented. Four comments elsewhere
that said "the five species" were already wrong and now say what is true.

And the board width, which the catalogue has always carried and /design has
never offered: 3 1/4 inch strip through 8 inch and wider. With ash that takes
the configurator from 100 combinations to 324, every one of them read from the
catalogue rather than typed here.

The species chips are now the photographed tile rather than two hex values on a
diagonal - the same tile the preview renders from, so the chip and the floor it
selects cannot show different wood.

65 of 66 guards pass. verify:css cannot run without an install and fails
identically on a clean checkout. parse-scan clean on 640 files.

